notes="""<h3>More on exchange rates and regimes</h3>
<ul>
<li>Traders quote <b>nominal</b> exchange rates. The <b>real</b> exchange rate adjusts for price levels: real (price/base) = nominal × (P<sub>base</sub> / P<sub>price</sub>). With no price changes, nominal and real changes are equal.</li>
<li>The real rate (domestic per foreign) <b>falls</b> when the domestic price level rises.</li>
<li>Percentage change of the base currency = new/old − 1. The price currency's change is old/new − 1.</li>
<li>Market participants: sell side (large dealing banks), buy side (corporations, real money and leveraged accounts, retail, governments, central banks).</li>
<li>Regimes that keep a national currency: fixed peg, target zone, crawling peg, crawling band, managed and free floats. Regimes that give it up: dollarization, monetary union. A currency board keeps the currency but backs it fully with reserves.</li>
<li>A conventional fixed peg allows the rate to fluctuate within about ±1% of the peg.</li>
<li>Devaluation improves the trade balance only if the <b>Marshall-Lerner</b> condition holds, and even then may first worsen it (<b>J-curve</b>). Under the <b>absorption</b> approach, lasting improvement requires more saving relative to investment.</li>
<li>Capital restrictions aim to keep domestic interest rates <b>low</b>, maintain fixed exchange rates, reduce asset price volatility and protect strategic industries.</li>
</ul>
<p class="f">real FX rate (d/f) = nominal (d/f) × CPI<sub>f</sub> / CPI<sub>d</sub></p>""",
